<section #sec:citations | Citations>

<sub-section | General Usage>

A citation points at a source by key with `<cite key>`. References are loaded once with `<library>` — paste entries straight from a reference manager — and the reference list is printed with `<bibliography>`. Citations resolve at build time against the loaded library.

<code-block #code:cite-basic>
<library format=bibtex>
@article{goodall2024, author={Jane Goodall}, title={Field Notes}, journal={Nature}, year={2024}}
</library>

The data supports this claim <cite goodall2024>.

<bibliography>
</bibliography>
</code-block>

The `<cite goodall2024>` marker renders as "(Goodall 2024)", and `<bibliography>` prints the full entry. Cite several sources at once by listing keys: `<cite smith2020 jones2021>`.

<sub-section | Arguments>

<table csv -numbered>
keys (one or more source keys), goodall2024  ·  smith2020 jones2021
library — format (the entry format), format=bibtex
library — src (load from an external file), src=references.bib
</table>
</section>

<section #sec:cross-references | Cross-References>

<sub-section | General Usage>

A cross-reference links to any numbered target — a figure, table, equation, theorem, or section — by its id, using the `@` reference sigil. The link text and number fill in automatically.

<code-block #code:ref-basic>
<fig #fig:scatter src=scatter.png | A scatter plot of the data.>

As shown in <ref @fig:scatter>, the trend is clear.
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:ref-basic>
<fig #fig:scatter src=scatter.png | A scatter plot of the data.>

As shown in <ref @fig:scatter>, the trend is clear.
</minipage>

<sub-section | Notes>

The `#` sigil *declares* an id (`<fig #fig:scatter>`); the `@` sigil *references* it (`<ref @fig:scatter>`). On screen a reference shows a hover preview of its target — add `-preview` to switch that off.
</section>

<section #sec:notes | Notes>

<sub-section | General Usage>

A note attaches a comment to the text — one note type with three positions. `<note>` (position `foot`/`end`) becomes a numbered footnote collected at the foot of the document; `<note position=margin>` places the same numbered note beside the text in the margin.

<code-block #code:notes-basic>
The result holds in general<note | With the usual regularity assumptions.>. A lighter
note can sit in the margin instead.<note position=margin | Margins suit short, glanceable remarks.>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:notes-basic>
The result holds in general<note | With the usual regularity assumptions.>. A lighter
note can sit in the margin instead.<note position=margin | Margins suit short, glanceable remarks.>
</minipage>

<sub-section | Notes>

Footnotes hover-preview their content on screen. Set `<config note-position=margin />` to render numbered notes as Tufte-style sidenotes instead of at the foot.
</section>

<section #sec:terms-glossaries | Defined Terms & Glossaries>

<sub-section | General Usage>

`<term>` marks a defined term inline where you introduce it. A `<glossary>` collects terms and their definitions, each in a `<glossary-entry>` built from a `<dt>` term and a `<dd>` definition.

<code-block #code:term-basic>
An <term | eigenvector> is a non-zero vector that scales under a transformation.

<glossary>
<glossary-entry #g:eigenvector>
<dt | Eigenvector>
<dd | A non-zero vector whose direction a transformation preserves.>
</glossary-entry>
</glossary>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:term-basic>
An <term | eigenvector> is a non-zero vector that scales under a transformation.

<glossary>
<glossary-entry #g:eigenvector>
<dt | Eigenvector>
<dd | A non-zero vector whose direction a transformation preserves.>
</glossary-entry>
</glossary>
</minipage>
</section>
